---
title: "Botter Session"
description: "Design an agent with the user, write its files, check them and build them into one .bot file."
---

STEP 1: UNDERSTAND
ASK "What agent should I build? Tell me what it should do, for whom, and anything it must never do. (Or ask me a question.)" into {request}
IF {request} is a question about Botter or agents rather than a request to build or change one:
    Answer the question in {request} briefly.
    RETURN "Ask me anything else, or describe an agent to build."
List this folder with $fs_list and keep the listing as {existing}.
IF {existing} contains "config.json" or {existing} contains "agent.md":
    Read config.json, agent.md, FLOW.fwl and the skills in this folder, and say in two sentences what this agent does now and which version it is.
Write one short message with at most 3 questions about purpose, users, limits and existing code that {request} leaves open, each with a proposed default, and call it {questions}. If nothing important is unclear, {questions} is the empty text "".
IF {questions} is not empty:
    ASK {questions} into {details}
ELSE:
    There are no further details: call that {details}.
IF {request} or {details} asks to wrap an existing repository or program:
    Follow only section 1 of #wrap-existing-project for {request} and {details}: put the code in artifacts/ and study it. Write no project files and run no builds. Keep your notes as {repo_notes}: what it does, entry points with their real arguments, inputs, outputs, what must be installed or running, network use, run time, licence, usage rules.
ELSE:
    There is no existing code: call that {repo_notes}.

STEP 2: PLAN
Plan the agent with #create-agent from {request}, {details} and {repo_notes}. Only plan in this step: write no files and run no builds. Keep the plan as {plan}, starting with Requirements: every input, behaviour, rule, limit and notification in the request and the details, each followed by the tool action, skill or FLOW.fwl step that implements it (nothing may be left out). Then the agent's name, its role, its skills, its tools (name, purpose, input JSON, what it calls), its FLOW.fwl steps, its state files and what must be installed or running.
ASK "That is my plan (above). Shall I build it? Say yes, or tell me what to change." into {verdict}
WHILE {verdict} is not an approval of the plan, at most 3 times:
    Revise the plan with the changes the user asked for in {verdict}, and keep the revised plan as {plan}.
    ASK "Here is the revised plan. Shall I build it?\n\n{plan}" into {verdict}
IF {verdict} is not an approval of the plan:
    RETURN "Stopped: the plan was not approved. Tell me what you want and we can start again."

STEP 3: WRITE
Write agent.md and the skills of {plan} as #write-agent-md-and-skills says.
List the tools in {plan} as a JSON array of objects with "name" and "purpose", or [] if it has none, and call it {tools}.
FOR EACH {tool} in {tools}:
    Write this tool as #write-tool says: {tool}
Test the project's tools with $tool_test and keep its report as {tested}.
WHILE {tested} contains "FAILED", at most 4 times:
    Fix the tools (or their "example") so that each FAILED one in this report works for real, then nothing else: {tested}
    Test the tools again with $tool_test and keep its report as {tested}.
Write config.json as the project-layout skill says: name (this folder's name), display_name, version (0.1.0 for a new agent; for a changed one, bump PATCH for fixes, MINOR for new abilities, MAJOR for changed behaviour), a one-sentence description, "internet": false only if {plan} says the agent must work offline, the skills and tools written above, and in "requires" every program they need on the machine.
IF {plan} describes more than one step for the agent:
    Write FLOW.fwl for {plan} as #write-flow says.
Review the project against {request}, {details} and {plan} as #review-agent says, and call the gaps you find {gaps}: a JSON array of texts, [] if there are none.
FOR EACH {gap} in {gaps}:
    Fix this gap, changing only what it needs and keeping every other action, step and rule that works: {gap}

STEP 4: CHECK
Read #build-agent before building {plan}.
Check the project with $agent_build, passing "dry_run": true, and keep its report as {check}.
WHILE {check} contains "error:" or {check} contains "warning:", at most 5 times:
    Fix every error and warning reported here: {check}
    Check the project again with $agent_build, passing "dry_run": true, and keep its report as {check}.
IF {check} contains "error:":
    RETURN the errors that remain in {check} and what the user can do about them
List this folder with $fs_list and keep the listing as {files}.
IF {files} contains "FLOW.fwl":
    Review the flow with $fwl_review and keep its verdict as {review}.
    IF {review.makes_sense} is false:
        ASK "Some steps in FLOW.fwl do not make sense yet:\n\n{review.report}\nShall I apply these suggestions?" [yes, no] into {apply}
        IF {apply} is "yes":
            Apply the suggestions in {review} to FLOW.fwl, then check the project with $agent_build, passing "dry_run": true.

STEP 5: SEAL
ASK "Last question before I build it. Should the agent carry its own keys?\n\nYes: I seal the keys it needs (the LLM key, and any MCP or A2A tokens) into the file behind a password you choose. After that, running the agent takes only that password. Good for an agent you run yourself.\n\nNo: the agent asks whoever runs it for their own LLM key, as usual. Good for an agent you will share." [yes, no] into {seal}
IF {seal} is "yes":
    Seal the agent's keys with $vault_seal.

STEP 6: BUILD
Build the agent with $agent_build, passing "dry_run": false, and keep its report as {build}.
Inspect the .bot file named in {build} with $agent_inspect and keep what it lists as {contents}.
RETURN the file name, its size, how to run it, what it asks at startup and what must be installed or running, from {build} and {contents}
